              "sourceUnprintedSupplementaryText": "3.1 Modern Periodic Table\nThe modern periodic table was put forward by Henry Gwyn Jeffreys Moseley, an English physicist. This table is based on the atomic number.\n\nThe modern periodic law states that: “The physical and chemical properties of the elements are the periodic function of their atomic number.”\n\nPeriodic table is formed on the basis of this law. In the simplified form it is divided into eight vertical columns known as groups / families and seven horizontal rows known as periods.\n\n3.1.1 Periods\nThe horizontal rows of elements in the periodic table are called periods. They are numbered from 1 to 7. From the table, we can see that all the elements in the same period have the same number of electronic shells. In the first, second and third period, the number of shells are 1, 2 and 3 respectively as shown in Fig. 3.1.\n\nScientific Information\n• The only letter that does not appear on the periodic table is J.\n• The rarest naturally-occurring element in the earth’s crust is Astatine. The entire crust appears to contain about 28 g of this element.\n\nFig. 3.1: Numbers of shells with correspondence to the period number.\n\nContinuation — textbook source, printed page 68:\nThere are seven periods (horizontal rows) in the periodic table.\n\n• 1st period contain two elements i.e. hydrogen (H) and Helium (He). This is also called the shortest period of the periodic table.\n• 2nd and 3rd periods each contains eight elements and are called short periods.\n• 4th and 5th periods have 18 elements each. In these elements, eight elements are called representative elements and the rest of 10 elements are called Transition elements. In 4th period it ranges from ₂₁Sc (scandium) to ₃₀Zn (zinc) and in 5th period it ranges from ₃₉Y (Yttrium) to ₄₈Cd (cadmium).\n• 6th period contains 32 elements, of which 8 are representative elements, 10 are transition elements and the rest 14 are known as Lanthanides series.\n• 7th period has also 8 representative elements, 10 transition element and the rest are called Actinides series.\n\nPeriodic table is still incomplete and with the discoveries of new elements, it will keep on adding these elements over the time. The number of valence electrons (electrons in the outermost shells) of the elements in the same period increases gradually by one, across the period from left to right. This leads to a parallel development of properties of elements in each period. For example, the metallic properties tend to decrease across each period while the non-metallic properties tend to increase.\n\nScientific Information\nRepresentative Elements: All s-block and p-block elements are called representative elements. Transition elements: Those elements which show variable valency are called transition elements. Lanthanides Series: Lanthanides series are those elements which come after lanthanum in 6th periods. The lanthanide series contain 14 elements, which ranges from ₅₈Ce (Cerium) to ₇₁Lu (Lutetium). Placed below the periodic table. Lanthanide series elements are also called rare earth elements. Actinide series are those elements which come after Actinium in 7th periods. The Actinide series contain 14 elements, which ranges from ₉₀Th (Thorium) to ₁₀₃Lr (Lawrencium), placed separately at the bottom of the periodic table beneath Lanthanide series.\n\nPractice Problem 3.1 (questions, printed page 69):\n• Which is the first element of the second period of the periodic table?\n• Which one is the last element of the second period of the periodic table?\n• How many elements are in that period?\n\nSpaceBook-authored answers (not printed beside these source questions): Lithium (Li); Neon (Ne); eight elements.\n\nSpaceBook expanded study notes (additional material retained pending its own page-by-page source match)::\n3.1 Modern Periodic Table:\nThe modern periodic table was put forward by Henry Gwyn Jeffreys Moseley (1913), an English physicist. This table is based on the atomic number (Z).\nThe Modern Periodic Law states that:\n'The physical and chemical properties of the elements are the periodic function of their atomic numbers.'\nPeriodic table is formed on the basis of this law. In the simplified form it is divided into eight vertical columns known as groups / families and seven horizontal rows known as periods.\n\n3.1.1 Periods:\nThe horizontal rows of elements in the periodic table are called periods. They are numbered from 1 to 7. All the elements in the same period have the same number of electronic shells. In the first, second and third period, the number of shells are 1, 2 and 3 respectively.\nFig 3.1: Numbers of shells correspond directly to the period number (Period 1 fills n=1, Period 2 fills n=2, Period 3 fills n=3).\n\nDetails of the 7 Periods:\n1. 1st Period (Shortest Period): Contains only 2 elements: Hydrogen (1H) and Helium (2He). Fills K-shell (1s).\n2. 2nd Period (Short Period): Contains 8 elements (Lithium 3Li to Neon 10Ne). Fills L-shell (2s, 2p).\n3. 3rd Period (Short Period): Contains 8 elements (Sodium 11Na to Argon 18Ar). Fills M-shell (3s, 3p).\n4. 4th Period (Long Period): Contains 18 elements (Potassium 19K to Krypton 36Kr). Contains 8 representative elements and 10 transition elements (21Sc to 30Zn).\n5. 5th Period (Long Period): Contains 18 elements (Rubidium 37Rb to Xenon 54Xe). Contains 8 representative elements and 10 transition elements (39Y to 48Cd).\n6. 6th Period (Very Long Period): Contains 32 elements (Caesium 55Cs to Radon 86Rn). Contains 8 representative elements, 10 transition elements, and 14 Lanthanides (58Ce to 71Lu).\n7. 7th Period (Incomplete / Long Period): Contains Francium 87Fr to Oganesson 118Og, containing 8 representative, 10 transition, and 14 Actinides (90Th to 103Lr).\n\nAcross a period from left to right, the number of valence electrons increases gradually by one, metallic character decreases, and non-metallic character increases.\n\nScientific Information:\n• The only letter that does not appear on the periodic table is J.\n• The rarest naturally-occurring element in the earth's crust is Astatine (At). The entire crust contains about 28 g.\n• Representative Elements: All s-block and p-block elements.\n• Transition Elements: d-block elements showing variable valency.\n• Lanthanides (Rare Earths): 14 elements (58Ce to 71Lu) following Lanthanum.\n• Actinides: 14 elements (90Th to 103Lr) following Actinium placed at bottom.\n\n",
