            "q": "(i) Which element of group IA is not an alkali metal and why?",
            "ans": "Answer:\nHydrogen (H) is the element in Group IA that is NOT an alkali metal.\nReasons:\n1. Non-Metallic Nature: Hydrogen is a diatomic non-metal gas (H2), whereas all other Group IA elements (Li, Na, K, Rb, Cs, Fr) are soft, highly reactive solid metals.\n2. Ionization Energy: Hydrogen has a very high ionization energy (1312 kJ/mol) compared to alkali metals (e.g. Li = 520 kJ/mol).\n3. Covalent Bonding: Hydrogen predominantly forms covalent bonds (e.g. H2O, CH4) and can also gain an electron to form a hydride ion (H-), behaving like a halogen.",
