              "desc": "Activity:5.2\n\nAim:\n\nTo demonstrate Charles's Law using simple materials.\n\nApparatus:\n\nGlass bottle/flask, balloon and hot plate.\n\nMethod:\n\n1. Place the balloon over the opening of the empty bottle/flask.\n\n2. Place the bottle/flask on the hot plate and allow it to heat up. Observe what happens to the balloon.\n\nWARNING: Be careful when handling the heated bottle/flask. You may need to wear gloves for protection.\n\nResults:\n\nYou should see that the balloon starts to expand. As the air inside the bottle/flask is heated, the pressure also increases, causing the volume to increase. Since the volume of the glass bottle/flask can't increase, the air moves into the balloon, causing it to expand.\n\nConclusion:\n\nThe temperature and volume of the gas are directly related to each other. As one increase, so does the other.\n\nLiquid State\n\nLiquid state is the state of matter in which the intermolecular forces of attraction are stronger than gaseous state. These forces of attraction is strong enough to hold the molecules together but not so strong to stop the molecular motions. Liquid molecules have kinetic energy, they move randomly. A liquid therefore, possess a fixed volume but no definite shape or form. The surface of a liquid is always leveled.\n\n5.3 Typical Properties of Liquids\n\nSome typical properties of liquid state are,\n\n(i) Volume and shape\n\nLiquid has a definite volume but no definite shape that is why it takes the shape of the container in which it is placed.\n\n(ii) Evaporation\n\nThe molecules of a liquid move with different kinetic energies. The molecules of the liquids whose kinetic energies are higher, move faster and overcome the intermolecular attractive forces. These molecules come out of the liquid and convert into the gaseous state. The conversion of liquid state into gaseous state is called evaporation. We can say that the phenomenon in which a liquid is converted into its vapours without external heating is called evaporation.\n\nFig 5.10 Process of Evaporation\n\nEvaporation takes places at all temperatures. When the molecules of higher kinetic energies are converted into the gaseous state and escape into the atmosphere; the temperature of the remaining liquid falls. That is why evaporation cause cooling.\n\nThe Evaporation depends on the following factors.\n\n(a) Surface area\n\nThe process of evaporation takes place from the surface. Greater the surface area higher will be the rate of evaporation and vice versa.\n\n(b) Temperature\n\nWith the increase in temperature, the average kinetic energy of the liquid molecules increases, so the rate of evaporation increases.\n\n(c) Intermolecular force\n\nThe evaporation of the liquids depends upon the intermolecular forces. Stronger the intermolecular forces slow will be the rate of evaporation and vice versa.\n\nDifferent liquids have different rate of evaporation at the same temperature, because the attractive forces are different in different liquids. For example, water has stronger intermolecular force than alcohol, therefore alcohol evaporate quickly than water.",
              "sciNote": "Evaporation is a continuous, spontaneous surface process occurring at all temperatures, whereas boiling occurs throughout the bulk liquid only at a specific temperature.",
              "sq": "Why does evaporation cause cooling, and what are the factors affecting it?",
              "mcq": {
                "q": "At 25°C (298 K), which of the following liquids exerts the highest vapour pressure?",
                "opts": [
                  "Chloroform (170 mm Hg)",
                  "Carbon tetrachloride (87 mm Hg)",
                  "Water (18 mm Hg)",
                  "Glycerol (0.00016 mm Hg)"
                ],
                "ans": 0
              },
              "sourceUnprintedSupplementaryText": "Liquid State:\nLiquid state is the state of matter in which the intermolecular forces of attraction are stronger than gaseous state. These forces of attraction are strong enough to hold the molecules together but not so strong to stop molecular motions. Liquid molecules have kinetic energy; they move randomly. A liquid therefore possesses a fixed volume but no definite shape or form. The surface of a liquid is always leveled.\n\n5.3 Typical Properties of Liquids:\n(i) Volume and shape:\nLiquid has a definite volume but no definite shape; that is why it takes the shape of the container in which it is placed.\n\n(ii) Evaporation:\nThe molecules of a liquid move with different kinetic energies. The molecules of the liquid whose kinetic energies are higher move faster and overcome the intermolecular attractive forces. These molecules come out of the liquid surface and convert into the gaseous state. The conversion of liquid state into gaseous state is called evaporation. We can say that the phenomenon in which a liquid is converted into its vapours without external heating is called evaporation.\n[Fig. 5.10 Process of Evaporation: High-energy surface molecules escape into the vapor phase].\n\nEvaporation takes place at all temperatures. When the molecules of higher kinetic energies escape into the atmosphere, the average kinetic energy of the remaining liquid falls, which lowers the temperature. That is why evaporation causes cooling.\n\nFactors affecting Evaporation:\n(a) Surface area: Evaporation is a surface phenomenon. Greater the surface area, higher will be the rate of evaporation.\n(b) Temperature: With increase in temperature, the average kinetic energy of molecules increases, so rate of evaporation increases.\n(c) Intermolecular forces: Stronger the intermolecular forces, slower will be the rate of evaporation (e.g. water has stronger hydrogen bonding than alcohol, so alcohol evaporates faster than water).\n\n(iii) Vapour Pressure:\nThe pressure exerted by the vapours of the liquid when the rate of evaporation becomes equal to rate of condensation is called vapour pressure.\n\nWhen a liquid is placed in a closed container, high-energy molecules escape from the surface (evaporation) and accumulate above the liquid. As vapour molecules accumulate, some strike back the liquid surface and re-enter the liquid (condensation). As evaporation proceeds, condensation rate increases until rate of evaporation = rate of condensation (Dynamic Equilibrium):\nLiquid ⇌ Vapours\n[Fig. 5.11 Vapour Pressure in a closed container].\n\nFactors affecting Vapour Pressure:\n(a) Nature of liquid: Polar liquids (stronger IMF, high boiling points) exert lower vapour pressure; non-polar liquids (weak IMF, low boiling points) exert higher vapour pressure (e.g. water has lower VP than acetone at same temperature).\n(b) Intermolecular forces: Stronger IMF leads to lower vapour pressure (e.g. VP of ethyl alcohol > water).\n(c) Size of molecules: Liquids with smaller molecules evaporate more easily and exert higher vapour pressure than larger molecules (e.g. pentane C5H12 exerts higher VP than decane C10H22).\n(d) Temperature: Increasing temperature increases kinetic energy and weakens IMF, rapidly increasing vapour pressure.\n\nTable 5.1: Some Compounds and their Vapour Pressure at 298 K (25°C):\n• Chloroform (CHCl3): 170 mm Hg\n• Carbon tetrachloride (CCl4): 87 mm Hg\n• Water (H2O): 18 mm Hg\n• Glycerol (C3H8O3): 0.00016 mm Hg",
