              "num": "6.1.1",
              "name": "Components of a Solution & Universal Solvent",
              "desc": "6.1 Solution, Aqueous Solution, Solute and Solvent\n\n(i) Solution\n\nA homogenous mixture of two or more than two substances is called solution. A solution is usually made up of two components i.e. solvent and solute. A solution, which is made of two components, is called binary solution. For example, salt dissolved in water.\n\nTest Yourself.\n\nSuggest a method for differentiation between pure liquid and solution.\n\n(ii) Aqueous Solution\n\nThe solution which is formed by dissolving a substance in water is called an aqueous solution. In aqueous solution, water is present in large amount and is termed as solvent. For example, salt in water and sugar in water. Water is polar covalent compound. Water has the ability to dissolve a large number of substances, that is why water is called as universal solvent. It dissolve majority of ionic and polar covalent substances.\n\n(iii) Solute and Solvent\n\nThe component of solution which is present in smaller amount is called solute and the component of solution which is present in larger amount is called solvent. A solute is dissolved in solvent to make solution. For example, sugar solution is made by dissolving sugar in water. In this solution, sugar is solute and water is solvent. Beside this, there are a number of solutions which consist of more than two substances. For example, soft drinks, in this water is solvent and sugar, carbon dioxide (CO₂), flavours etc. are the solutes.\n\nFig 6.1 Solvent and solute combine to form solution",
              "sciNote": "Water's high dielectric constant (~80) and strong permanent dipole moment make it uniquely capable of overcoming ionic lattice energies and hydrating cations and anions.",
              "sq": "Why is water regarded as a universal solvent?",
              "mcq": {
                "q": "In a soft drink, which component acts as the solvent?",
                "opts": [
                  "Water",
                  "Carbon dioxide gas",
                  "Sugar",
                  "Flavouring syrup"
                ],
                "ans": 0
              },
              "sourceUnprintedSupplementaryText": "(i) Solution:\nA homogeneous mixture of two or more than two substances is called a solution. A solution is usually made up of two components i.e. solvent and solute. A solution, which is made of two components, is called a binary solution. For example, salt dissolved in water.\n\nTest Yourself (source prompt):\nSuggest a method for differentiation between pure liquid and solution.\nSpaceBook-authored answer: Perform a simple distillation or evaporation test: when heated to boiling, a pure liquid boils at a single fixed, sharp boiling point (e.g. pure water at 100°C) and evaporates completely leaving no residue. In contrast, a solution boils over a temperature range and leaves behind a solid residue of solute (such as salt crystals).\n\n(ii) Aqueous Solution:\nThe solution which is formed by dissolving a substance in water is called an aqueous solution. In an aqueous solution, water is present in large amount and is termed as the solvent. For example, salt in water and sugar in water. Water is a polar covalent compound. Water has the ability to dissolve a large number of substances, that is why water is called the universal solvent. It dissolves the majority of ionic and polar covalent substances.\n\n(iii) Solute and Solvent:\nThe component of a solution which is present in smaller amount is called the solute, and the component of a solution which is present in larger amount is called the solvent. A solute is dissolved in a solvent to make a solution. For example, sugar solution is made by dissolving sugar in water. In this solution, sugar is the solute and water is the solvent.\n\nBeside this, there are a number of solutions which consist of more than two substances. For example, in soft drinks, water is the solvent while sugar, carbon dioxide (CO2), flavourings, and food acids are the solutes.\n[Fig. 6.1 Solvent and solute combine to form solution].",
