              "name": "Gaseous, Liquid, and Solid Solutions (9 Combinations)",
              "desc": "6.3 Types of Solution\n\nSolutions exist in gas, liquid and solid states. On this basis, there are nine types of solutions.\n\n6.3.1 Gaseous Solution (Gas in Gas, Liquid in Gas and Solid in Gas)\n\nDifferent types of gaseous solution are following\n\nFig. 6.3: Carbon dioxide in water\n\n6.3.2 Liquid Solutions (Gas in Liquid, Liquid in Liquid, Solid in Liquid)\n\nDifferent types of liquid solutions are following:\n\nFig. 6.4 Mercury Amalgam\n\nAmalgam Contains 50% Mercury\n\n6.3.3 Solids Solutions (Gas in Solid, Liquid in Solid, Solid in Solid)\n\nDifferent types of solid solution are following:\n\nBrass is an alloy of copper and zinc.\n\nSterling silver: 92.5% silver 7.5% copper\n\nBronze is an alloy of copper and tin.\n\nFig. 6.5 Brass, Sterling silver and Bronze alloys",
              "sciNote": "Alloys are homogeneous solid solutions of two or more metals (or a metal and non-metal) that exhibit metallic properties and enhanced tensile strength.",
              "sq": "Name the solute and solvent in: (a) Dental amalgam, (b) Air, (c) Brass, (d) Fog.",
              "mcq": {
                "q": "Dental amalgam used for tooth fillings is an example of which type of solution?",
                "opts": [
                  "Liquid in solid (Mercury in Silver)",
                  "Solid in liquid",
                  "Solid in solid",
                  "Gas in solid"
                ],
                "ans": 0
              },
              "sourceUnprintedSupplementaryText": "Solutions exist in gas, liquid, and solid states. On this basis, there are nine types of solutions:\n\n6.3.1 Gaseous Solutions:\n1. Gas in Gas: Solute = Oxygen (gas), Solvent = Nitrogen (gas) ── Example: Air.\n2. Liquid in Gas: Solute = Water vapours (liquid), Solvent = Air (gas) ── Example: Fog, mist, humidity in air.\n3. Solid in Gas: Solute = Carbon particles (solid), Solvent = Air (gas) ── Example: Smoke (carbon particles dispersed in air).\n[Fig. 6.3 Carbon dioxide dissolved in water in carbonated drinks].\n\n6.3.2 Liquid Solutions:\n4. Gas in Liquid: Solute = Carbon dioxide (gas), Solvent = Water (liquid) ── Example: Carbonated soft drinks, dissolved oxygen in river water.\n5. Liquid in Liquid: Solute = Alcohol (liquid), Solvent = Water (liquid) ── Example: Alcohol in water, vinegar (acetic acid in water).\n6. Solid in Liquid: Solute = Salts / Sugar (solid), Solvent = Water (liquid) ── Example: Sea water, sugar syrup, brine.\n\n6.3.3 Solid Solutions:\n7. Gas in Solid: Solute = Hydrogen (gas), Solvent = Palladium (solid) ── Example: Hydrogen gas adsorbed on palladium metal.\n8. Liquid in Solid: Solute = Mercury (liquid), Solvent = Silver / Gold / Copper (solid) ── Example: Dental amalgam (Hg in Ag/Sn) used by dentists (Fig. 6.4).\n9. Solid in Solid: Solute = Zinc (solid), Solvent = Copper (solid) ── Example: Brass alloy (copper + zinc), Bronze alloy (copper + tin), Sterling silver (92.5% silver + 7.5% copper) (Fig. 6.5).\n\nSociety, Technology and Science:\nThe substances we use in our lives for existence are solutions:\n• Gaseous solution: Air is a solution of nitrogen, oxygen, argon, carbon dioxide, and noble gases.\n• Liquid solution: Drinking water contains vital dissolved mineral ions; beverages, vinegar, shampoos, glass cleaners, and liquid medicines are liquid solutions.\n• Solid solution: Alloys provide structural integrity and beauty. Pure gold is too soft for jewelry, so it is alloyed with copper or silver. Brass and stainless steel are used for surgical tools, car parts, and utensils. Aircraft frames are constructed from high-strength light solid solutions of Aluminium and Magnesium (Duralumin). Dental fillings use dental amalgam (mercury dissolved in silver).",
