              "num": "6.6.2",
              "name": "Suspensions (Turbidity) & Comparative Properties Table",
              "desc": "The distinctive properties of solutions, colloids and suspensions are summarized in Table 6.2\n\nTable 6.2 Properties of Solutions, Colloids and Suspensions\n\nSolutions: Table salt in water is an example of solution.\n\nColloids: Milk in water is an example of colloids.\n\nSuspensions: Flour in water is an example of suspensions.\n\nFig. 6.14: Comparison of solution, colloids and suspension",
              "sciNote": "Medicinal syrups like antacids are suspensions; shaking re-disperses settled particles uniformly to ensure accurate dosage.",
              "sq": "Why is it necessary to shake suspension medicines (such as antacids) thoroughly before use?",
              "mcq": {
                "q": "Which mixture can be separated into its components using ordinary filter paper?",
                "opts": [
                  "Suspension (Chalk in water)",
                  "True solution (Salt in water)",
                  "Colloid (Milk)",
                  "Gelatin in water"
                ],
                "ans": 0
              },
              "sourceUnprintedSupplementaryText": "6.6.3 Suspension (Turbidity):\nA suspension is a heterogeneous mixture of undissolved visible solid particles dispersed in a liquid or gas medium. Its composition is non-uniform. In a suspension, the solute particles are large with diameters greater than 1000 nm (>1 μm) and can be seen easily with the naked eye.\n\nSedimentation and Decantation:\nWhen left undisturbed, suspended particles settle down at the bottom of the container due to gravity; this settling process is called sedimentation. The clear supernatant liquid at the top can then be poured off in a process called decantation. Particles in a suspension cannot pass through filter paper and are retained as residue during filtration.\n\nExamples of Suspensions:\n• Muddy water, chalk in water, flour in water, milk of magnesia (magnesium hydroxide suspension).\n• Pharmaceutical suspensions: Antacid syrups, liquid antibiotics, and paracetamol suspensions, which carry the mandatory label: 'Shake well before use'.\n[Fig. 6.13 Suspension and Solution; Fig. 6.14 Comparison of Solution, Colloid, and Suspension].\n\nTable 6.2: Properties of Solutions, Colloids, and Suspensions:\n1. Homogeneity: Solution = Homogeneous | Colloid = Heterogeneous | Suspension = Heterogeneous.\n2. Particle Size: Solution = 0.01 - 1 nm (atoms, ions, small molecules) | Colloid = 1 - 1000 nm (macromolecules or aggregates) | Suspension = Over 1000 nm (large visible particles).\n3. Settling (Stability): Solution = Do not separate on standing | Colloid = Do not separate on standing | Suspension = Particles settle down on standing (sedimentation).\n4. Filtration: Solution = Cannot be separated by filtration | Colloid = Cannot be separated by filtration | Suspension = Can be completely separated by filtration.\n5. Light Scattering (Tyndall Effect): Solution = Do not scatter light (transparent) | Colloid = Scatter light (Tyndall effect, translucent/opaque) | Suspension = May scatter/block light but is not transparent (opaque).\n6. Particle Visibility: Solution = Cannot be seen with naked eye or microscope | Colloid = Cannot be seen with naked eye | Suspension = Big enough to be seen with naked eye.",
