              "sourceUnprintedSupplementaryText": "7.5 Electrochemical Cell:\nA device in which interconversion of electrical and chemical energy takes place is called an electrochemical cell. Electrochemical cells are of two types: (i) Electrolytic cell, (ii) Voltaic / Galvanic cell.\n\n7.5.1 Concept of Electrolytes:\nAn electrolyte is a substance which dissociates or ionizes in aqueous solution or molten state into positive and negative ions and conducts electricity (Fig. 7.3).\n(i) Strong Electrolytes: Ionize completely in aqueous solution or molten state and conduct electricity extensively. Examples: H2SO4, HNO3, HCl, NaCl, NaOH, NaNO3.\n(ii) Weak Electrolytes: Ionize partially/slightly and conduct electricity to a small extent. Examples: Acetic acid (CH3COOH), Ammonium hydroxide (NH4OH), Benzoic acid.\n(iii) Non-Electrolytes: Do not ionize in solution or molten state and do not conduct electricity. Examples: Benzene, Sugar, Urea, Glucose.\n\nElectrodes: Metallic conductors (plates, rods, wires) through which electrons enter or leave the cell:\n• Anode: Positive electrode in electrolytic cells where oxidation occurs (anions lose electrons).\n• Cathode: Negative electrode in electrolytic cells where reduction occurs (cations gain electrons).\n\n7.5.2 Electrolytic Cells:\nAn electrolytic cell uses an external electrical source (battery) to drive a non-spontaneous redox reaction in a single container (Fig. 7.4).\n\nElectrolysis of Molten NaCl:\n2NaCl(l) ──> 2Na+(l) + 2Cl-(l)\n• Anode (Oxidation): 2Cl-(l) ──> Cl2(g) + 2e-\n• Cathode (Reduction): 2Na+(l) + 2e- ──> 2Na(l)\n• Overall Reaction: 2NaCl(l) ──> 2Na(l) + Cl2(g)\n\nElectrolytic Refining of Copper (Fig. 7.5):\nPure copper (99.99%) is essential for electrical applications. Large blocks of impure blister copper are made the Anode, thin pure copper sheets are made the Cathode, and an acidified solution of CuSO4 + H2SO4 acts as the Electrolyte (at 50°C and an applied voltage of about 0.3 V; the optimum current density is 160–400 A/m²):\n• Anode (Oxidation): Cu(impure s) ──> Cu2+(aq) + 2e-\n• Cathode (Reduction): Cu2+(aq) + 2e- ──> Cu(pure deposited s)\nActive impurities (Zn, Fe) dissolve as ions, while precious noble impurities (Gold, Silver, Platinum) settle under the anode as valuable 'Anode Mud' / 'Anode Sludge'.\n\nElectrodes are the conductors i.e. metallic plates, wires or rods, through which electrons enter or leave the electrolytes in a cell. There are two types of electrodes: anode and cathode.\n(a) Anode: The anode is the positive electrode at which the anion gathers and leaves the electron in the electrolytic cell. Anions are negatively charged particles, e.g. Cl−, OH−.\n(b) Cathode: The cathode is the negative electrode at which the cation gathers and gains the electrons in the electrolytic cell. Cations are positively charged particles, e.g. Na+, NH4+.\n\nScientific Information (printed): Passing an electrical current through a substance may produce a temporary change such as lighting an electrical bulb, or a permanent change such as electroplating. Gold and silver are the best electrical conductors, but due to their high cost most electrical wires are made of copper and aluminium.",
