              "name": "Galvanic Cells, Daniell Cell & Batteries (Dry Cell)",
              "desc": "7.5.3 Galvanic Cells (Voltaic Cell)\n\nIf the redox reaction in an electrochemical cell occurs spontaneously and produces electrical energy, such a cell is called voltaic cell. The device, in which chemical energy (from the reactions between the electrodes and the solution) is converted into electrical energy, is known as the Galvanic cell. The best example of voltaic or galvanic cell is the Daniel cell as shown in fig 7.6.\n\nFig. 7.6: Daniel cell\n\nConstruction of a Daniel Cell\n\nA galvanic cell consists of two separate containers, each container is called as half cell. In each half cell, an electrode is dipped in 1M solution of its own salt. As shown in figure 7.6. The right half-cell consists of zinc electrode dipped in 1M solution of zinc sulphate (ZnSO₄). The left half-cell is a copper electrode dipped in 1M solution of copper sulphate (CuSO₄) and connected to a wire to an external circuit to which a galvanometer or voltmeter is attached. The solutions in different containers are connected with bridge. This bridge is known as ‘Salt Bridge’. A Salt Bridge is U-shaped tube. This tube is filled with electrolyte gel, such as K₂SO₄ or Na₂SO₄ solution and is called as the ‘agar’. The salt bridge inter-connects the two solutions in the anode container and the cathode container. A Salt Bridge performs three functions,\n\ni. It allows electrical contact between the two solutions.\n\nii. It prevents the mixing of the two solutions.\n\niii. It keeps electrical neutrality in each half cell.\n\nWorking of the cell\n\nThe Zn metal has tendency to lose electrons more readily than Copper when the circuit is completed. As a result, oxidation takes place at Zn electrode. The electrons flow from Zn-electrode through the external circuit to Copper electrode. These electrons are gained by the copper ions of the solution at cathode and deposited as copper atoms at the cathode.\n\nCell Reaction\n\nThe flow of electrons from one electrode to the other in the cell is due to the half cell reaction taking place in the anode and cathode compartments. The net chemical changes obtained by adding the two half cell reactions are called the cell reaction. Thus, we have half cell reactions i.e. oxidation and reduction processes, going on two electrodes simultaneously. Electrons travel in external circuit, while ions move through the salt bridge and in this way electric current is produced. These reactions are as follows:\n\n(i) Half-cell reaction at anode (Oxidation)\n\nZn(s) → Zn²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻\n\n(ii) Half-cell reaction at cathode (Reduction)\n\nCu²⁺(aq) + 2e⁻ → Cu(s)\n\nOverall galvanic reaction (the sum of two half-cell reactions)\n\nZn(s) + Cu²⁺(aq) → Zn²⁺(aq) + Cu(s)\n\nThe batteries which are used in automobiles, calculators, toys etc and to light the bulbs, work on the same principle.\n\nTable 7.2: Differences between an Electrolytic Cell and Galvanic Cell\n\nBattery\n\nA group or combination of galvanic cells joined in series is called battery. Car batteries consist of six or more identical voltaic cells connected in series. A battery is a self-contained, chemical power pack that can produce a limited amount of electrical energy, wherever it is needed. A battery converts chemicals energy into electrical energy, for specific period of time.\n\nFig.7.7: Many common batteries are simple voltaic dry cells\n\nDry cell:\n\nThe dry cell was prepared by LECLANCHE in 1887.\n\nConstruction of the Cell:\n\nThe dry cell consists of metallic container. Its container is made up of Zinc (Zn). This container acts as anode. This Zinc casing is consumed during the chemical reaction. A graphite rod is placed in the center of the container. This graphite rod acts as cathode. This container is filled with the mixture of Ammonium chloride (NH₄Cl), Manganese Dioxide (MnO₂) and Carbon (C). This mixture is in the paste form. The cell is water proofed with the wax. The voltage produced by the dry cell is 1.25V.\n\nReaction in the Cell\n\nOxidation and reduction reactions occur in the cell to produce the electric current.\n\nReaction at Anode\n\nThe Zinc acts as Anode in the cell. The Zinc is oxidized by losing the two electrons.\n\nZn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻\n\nFig. 7.8 Internal structure of dry cell\n\nAnode (Zinc Inner Case); Cathode (Graphite Rod); Paste of MnO₂, NH₄Cl and Carbon\n\nReaction at Cathode\n\nThe graphite acts as Cathode in the cell. In the cell the NH₄Cl and MnO₂ are reduced to Mn₂O₃ and NH₃.\n\n2MnO₂ + 2NH₄⁺ + 2e⁻ → Mn₂O₃ + 2NH₃ + H₂O\n\n2NH₄Cl → 2NH₄⁺ + 2Cl⁻\n\nOverall reaction\n\nZn²⁺ + 2Cl⁻ → ZnCl₂",
              "sciNote": "The standard electromotive force (EMF) generated by a Daniell cell is exactly 1.10 Volts under standard conditions (1.0 M solutions at 25°C).",
              "sq": "State the three essential functions of a salt bridge in a galvanic cell.",
              "mcq": {
                "q": "What is the voltage produced by a standard Daniell Cell?",
                "opts": [
                  "1.10 V",
                  "1.50 V",
                  "2.00 V",
                  "0.76 V"
                ],
                "ans": 0
              },
              "sourceUnprintedSupplementaryText": "7.5.3 Galvanic Cells (Voltaic Cells):\nA voltaic or galvanic cell is a device in which a spontaneous redox reaction produces electrical energy (converts chemical energy into electrical energy). The classic example is the Daniell Cell (Fig. 7.6).\n\nConstruction of Daniell Cell:\n• Anode Half-Cell: Zinc metal strip immersed in 1.0 M ZnSO4 solution.\n• Cathode Half-Cell: Copper metal strip immersed in 1.0 M CuSO4 solution.\n• Salt Bridge: A U-shaped glass tube containing an electrolyte gel (K2SO4, Na2SO4, or KCl in agar-agar gel) connecting the two solutions.\n\nThree Functions of a Salt Bridge:\n1. Provides electrical contact between the two half-cell solutions.\n2. Prevents mechanical mixing of the two separate solutions.\n3. Maintains electrical neutrality in each half-cell by migrating anions to the anode and cations to the cathode.\n\nWorking and Cell Reactions:\nZinc has a higher oxidation tendency than Copper. Zinc atoms lose electrons at the anode, which travel through the external circuit (lighting a bulb or showing 1.10 V on voltmeter) to the copper cathode where Cu2+ ions gain them:\n• Anode Reaction (Oxidation): Zn(s) ──> Zn2+(aq) + 2e-  [E° = +0.76 V]\n• Cathode Reaction (Reduction): Cu2+(aq) + 2e- ──> Cu(s)  [E° = +0.34 V]\n• Overall Cell Reaction: Zn(s) + Cu2+(aq) ──> Zn2+(aq) + Cu(s)  [Total EMF = 1.10 V]\n\nComparison between Voltaic Cell and Electrolytic Cell:\n1. Energy conversion: Voltaic = Chemical to Electrical | Electrolytic = Electrical to Chemical.\n2. Spontaneity: Voltaic = Spontaneous | Electrolytic = Non-spontaneous.\n3. Container: Voltaic = Two separate half-cells joined by salt bridge | Electrolytic = Single tank, no salt bridge.\n4. Anode polarity: Voltaic = Negative (-) | Electrolytic = Positive (+).\n5. Cathode polarity: Voltaic = Positive (+) | Electrolytic = Negative (-).\n\nBatteries & Dry Cell (Leclanché Cell, 1887, Fig. 7.7 & 7.8):\nA battery is a group of galvanic cells connected in series. A dry cell produces 1.25 - 1.5 V:\n• Anode: Zinc outer casing: Zn(s) ──> Zn2+(aq) + 2e-\n• Cathode: Central graphite rod surrounded by a moist paste of NH4Cl, MnO2, and carbon black.\n• Cathode Reaction: 2MnO2 + 2NH4+ + 2e- ──> Mn2O3 + 2NH3 + H2O\n• Overall Reaction: Zn + 2MnO2 + 2NH4Cl ──> ZnCl2 + Mn2O3 + 2NH3 + H2O.\n\nPrinted dry-cell reaction detail (source): The cell voltage given in this section is 1.25 V. Zinc oxidation: Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻. Cathode: 2MnO₂ + 2NH₄⁺ + 2e⁻ → Mn₂O₃ + 2NH₃ + H₂O. Ammonium chloride dissociates: 2NH₄Cl → 2NH₄⁺ + 2Cl⁻. Overall source equation: Zn²⁺ + 2Cl⁻ → ZnCl₂.",
