      "sloSq": [
        {
          "q": "1. (SLO) Why does a zinc strip placed in a blue copper sulphate solution cause the blue color to fade over time?",
          "ans": "Zinc is more electropositive and active than Copper. Zinc atoms oxidize and dissolve into solution as colorless Zn2+ ions (Zn ──> Zn2+ + 2e-), while blue Cu2+ ions in solution gain these electrons and deposit as reddish-brown solid copper on the zinc strip (Cu2+ + 2e- ──> Cu). As Cu2+ ions are consumed, the blue color of the solution gradually fades to colorless ZnSO4."
        },
        {
          "q": "2. (SLO) Explain why rusting does not take place in boiled distilled water sealed under a layer of oil.",
          "ans": "Boiling distilled water expels all dissolved oxygen gas (O2), and the layer of oil on top prevents atmospheric oxygen from re-dissolving. Since rusting is an electrochemical redox process that strictly requires BOTH water and oxygen (O2 acts as the cathodic electron acceptor), iron cannot rust in the absence of dissolved oxygen."
        },
        {
          "q": "3. (SLO) Why are tin-plated food cans safe for food storage, but once scratched, the iron underneath rusts faster than unplated iron?",
          "ans": "Tin is non-toxic and resists weak food acids, protecting the steel can. However, Iron is more active (more electropositive) than Tin. If the tin coating is scratched, an electrochemical cell is created where iron acts as the sacrificial anode and tin acts as the cathode. Consequently, iron oxidizes and rusts even faster than it would on its own."
        },
        {
          "q": "4. (SLO) Differentiate between the Anode in an Electrolytic Cell and the Anode in a Galvanic (Voltaic) Cell.",
          "ans": "• Electrolytic Cell Anode: Connected to the positive (+) terminal of an external power source; attracts anions and is the site of oxidation.\n• Galvanic Cell Anode: The negative (-) terminal of the cell because spontaneous oxidation generates a buildup of electrons on this electrode before they flow to the cathode."
        }
      ],
      "sloLq": [
        {
          "q": "1. (SLO) Comprehensive Investigation: Compare the construction, working principles, electrode half-reactions, and industrial significance of the Downs Cell (Sodium extraction) and Nelson Cell (Chlor-alkali process).",
          "ans": "1. Electrolyte Used:\n• Downs Cell: Fused (molten) anhydrous NaCl with CaCl2 flux at 600°C.\n• Nelson Cell: Concentrated aqueous NaCl solution (Brine) at room temperature.\n\n2. Electrode Construction:\n• Downs Cell: Central graphite anode emerging from the bottom with a collection dome; cylindrical iron cathode surrounding anode, separated by an iron wire gauze screen.\n• Nelson Cell: Graphite anode suspended in a U-shaped perforated steel tube cathode lined with a porous asbestos/polymer diaphragm.\n\n3. Anode Reactions (Oxidation):\n• Downs Cell: 2Cl-(l) ──> Cl2(g) + 2e- (Chlorine gas collected at dome).\n• Nelson Cell: 2Cl-(aq) ──> Cl2(g) + 2e- (Chlorine gas collected at outlet).\n\n4. Cathode Reactions (Reduction):\n• Downs Cell: 2Na+(l) + 2e- ──> 2Na(l) (Molten sodium metal floats and is tapped off).\n• Nelson Cell: 2H+(aq) + 2e- ──> H2(g) (H+ discharges preferentially over Na+; H2 gas collected at outlet).\n\n5. Solution / By-Product Formation:\n• Downs Cell: Cl2 gas is the sole by-product of metallic sodium extraction.\n• Nelson Cell: Unreduced Na+ combines with OH- to form aqueous NaOH (caustic soda), dripping into the catch basin; Cl2 and H2 are valuable co-products.\n\n6. Industrial Significance:\n• Downs Cell is the principal global route for metallic sodium (used in sodium vapor lamps, tetraethyl lead, and chemical synthesis).\n• Nelson Cell is the cornerstone chlor-alkali process producing caustic soda (soap, paper, textiles), chlorine (water disinfection, PVC), and hydrogen (ammonia synthesis, fuel)."
        }
      ],
