              "sourceUnprintedSupplementaryText": "8.1 Metals:\nMetals are those substances which are good conductors of heat and electricity. They are malleable and ductile. Their oxides and hydroxides are bases. All metals, except Mercury (Hg) which is liquid, exist in the solid state at room temperature.\n\nA metal is one which reacts with oxygen to produce a basic oxide. When dissolved in water, it forms an alkaline solution that turns red litmus paper blue.\n\nElectronic Definition of Metals:\nScientists redefined metals on the basis of loss and gain of electrons. A metal is an element which loses one or more electrons to form a positive ion (cation):\n• Na ──> Na+ + e-\n• Ca ──> Ca2+ + 2e-\n• Al ──> Al3+ + 3e-\nMetals are electropositive and have a strong tendency to lose valence electrons. The only exception to this definition is the non-metal Hydrogen, which can act as an electron donor (H+).\n\nScientific Tidbit (Distribution in Periodic Table):\n• Group 1 = All are metals except Hydrogen\n• Group 2 = All are metals\n• Group 3 = All are metals except Boron\n• Group 4 = Tin (Sn) and Lead (Pb) are metals\n• Group 5 = Antimony (Sb) and Bismuth (Bi) are metals\n• Group 6, 7, 8 = All are non-metals\n• d-Block Elements, Lanthanides & Actinides = All are metals\n\nScientific Information:\nThe earth is made up of about 8% Aluminium, 5% Iron, 4% Calcium, and less than 4% total of Potassium, Sodium, and Magnesium.\n\n8.1.1 Electropositive or Metallic Character:\nThe tendency of an element to lose electrons and form positive ions (cations) is called electropositive or metallic character. Elements with lower ionization energies have higher electropositive character (Alkali metals are the most electropositive).\n\n(i) Variation across a Period (Left to Right):\nMetallic character decreases across a period from left to right as nuclear charge increases and atomic radius decreases:\n• Period 2: Li, Be (Metals) ──> B (Metalloid) ──> C, N, O, F, Ne (Non-metals)\n• Period 3: Na, Mg, Al (Metals) ──> Si (Metalloid) ──> P, S, Cl, Ar (Non-metals)\n\n(ii) Variation down a Group (Top to Bottom):\nMetallic character increases down a group as atomic size and shielding effect increase, making valence electron removal easier:\n• Group IV: C (Non-metal) ──> Si, Ge (Metalloids) ──> Sn, Pb (Metals)\n• Group V: N, P (Non-metals) ──> As, Sb (Metalloids) ──> Bi (Metal)\n\nScientific Information (Conductance / Electrolysis Test):\nPrinted page222: Metals are good conductors of electricity while non-metals are insulators except carbon in the form of graphite. The electrolysis test consists of dissolving the element in an acid and passing an electric current through the solution. Metallic atoms become positively charged and move towards the negative pole or cathode of the electrolytic cell.\nDissolving an element in acid and passing an electric current causes metallic cations to migrate toward the negative cathode:\nZn(s) + 2HCl(aq) ──> ZnCl2(aq) + H2(g) ──> Zn2+(aq) + 2Cl-(aq)\n\nPrinted page 220 — Non-metals have the ability to accept electron(s) in their valence shell to form negatively charged particles called anions: F + e− → F−; Cl + e− → Cl−; O + 2e− → O²−. Elements having lower ionization energies have a higher tendency to lose electrons and therefore have electropositive or metallic behaviour. Alkali metals are the most highly electropositive elements; elements with high ionization energies are non-metals. Borderline elements behave as metalloids.",
