              "sourceUnprintedSupplementaryText": "Sodium (Na, At. No = 11, At. Mass = 22.99 g/mol, Fig. 8.2):\nPosition in Periodic Table: Period 3, Group IA (electronic configuration: 2, 8, 1).\n\nTable 8.4: Physical Properties of Sodium:\n1. Appearance: Silvery white metallic luster (freshly cut; rapidly tarnishes in air).\n2. Softness: Very soft metal; can be easily cut with a butter knife.\n3. Density: 0.971 g/cm^3 (lighter than water; floats on water).\n4. Melting Point: 97.6°C | Boiling Point: 880°C.\n5. Tensile Strength: Low tensile strength.\n6. Conductivity: Excellent conductor of heat and electricity.\n\nChemical Properties of Sodium:\n1. Reaction with Hydrogen: Forms ionic sodium hydride:\n   2Na(s) + H2(g) ──> 2NaH(s)\n2. Reaction with Oxygen: Burns with a golden-yellow flame forming basic sodium oxide which dissolves in water to form caustic soda (NaOH):\n   4Na(s) + O2(g) ──> 2Na2O(s)\n   Na2O(s) + H2O(l) ──> 2NaOH(aq)\n3. Reaction with Water: Vigorous exothermic reaction liberating hydrogen:\n   2Na(s) + 2H2O(l) ──> 2NaOH(aq) + H2(g) + Heat\n4. Reaction with Halogens: Forms sodium halides:\n   2Na(s) + Cl2(g) ──> 2NaCl(s)\n   2Na(s) + Br2(l) ──> 2NaBr(s)\n5. Reaction with Sulphur: Forms sodium sulphide:\n   2Na(s) + S(s) ──> Na2S(s)\n6. Powerful Reducing Agent: Reduces metal oxides and chlorides to extract pure metals:\n   2Na(s) + MgO(s) ──> Na2O(s) + Mg(s)\n   4Na(s) + TiCl4(l) ──> 4NaCl(s) + Ti(s)\n\nUses of Sodium:\ni. Preparation of essential chemicals: Sodium carbonate (Na2CO3), sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO3), caustic soda (NaOH), sodamide (NaNH2), and sodium cyanide (NaCN).\nii. Used in sodium vapour discharge lamps (bright orange-yellow light) for street and highway lighting.\niii. Used as a liquid metal heat-exchange coolant in fast nuclear reactors.\niv. Used in petroleum refining for desulphurization (removing sulphur impurities).\nv. Used as an industrial reducing agent to extract Titanium (Ti) and Zirconium (Zr) from their tetrachlorides.\nvi. Forms useful alloys, notably Sodium Amalgam (Na-Hg) and with silver.\n\nPrinted page229: Moving from alkali metals to alkaline earth metals across a period, melting and boiling points, densities and hardness increase, while electropositivity, conductivity, reducing power and atomic radii decrease. Sodium does not occur free because it is very reactive. It occurs in the sea as sodium chloride, sodium bromide and sodium iodide, and in deposits as rock salt. Freshly cut alkali metals show a shiny silvery surface that rapidly tarnishes in air. Sodium is stored in kerosene oil or liquid paraffin to avoid contact with air (oxygen and water).",
