            "q": "III. Discuss the reasons why some elements exist as free elements in nature while other occurs in combined states as compounds. Give two examples of each type.",
            "ans": "1. Scientific Reasons for Free vs Combined Existence:\n\nA. Elements Occurring in Combined State:\n• Causes: Elements that possess very low ionization energies (high electropositivity) or high electron affinities/electronegativity have incomplete valence shells and high chemical reactivity. They spontaneously lose, gain, or share electrons with surrounding oxygen, water, sulphur, or halogens to achieve stable noble gas octets and lower potential energy.\n• Examples:\n  1. Sodium (Na): Highly reactive alkali metal; exists as rock salt (NaCl), soda ash (Na2CO3), and Glauber's salt (Na2SO4·10H2O).\n  2. Calcium (Ca): Highly reactive alkaline earth metal; exists as limestone/marble (CaCO3) and gypsum (CaSO4·2H2O).\n\nB. Elements Occurring in Free (Native) State:\n• Causes: Elements that have completely filled valence electron shells (Noble gases) or very high ionization energies with low electropositivity and positive standard reduction potentials (Noble metals) do not readily react with oxygen, moisture, or environmental acids. They are thermodynamically stable in elemental form.\n• Examples:\n  1. Gold (Au): Noble precious metal that resists oxidation, corrosion, and acids, found as pure gold nuggets.\n  2. Helium / Argon (He, Ar): Noble gases with complete duplets/octets, existing as uncombined monoatomic atmospheric gases.",
