            "q": "IV. Define metal and non-metal and compare the properties (both physical and chemical) of metals and non-metals.",
            "ans": "1. Definitions:\n• Metal: An electropositive element that readily loses valence electrons to form cations, forms basic oxides, and is a lustrous conductor of heat and electricity.\n• Non-metal: An electronegative element that gains electrons to form anions, forms acidic oxides, and is a non-conductor/insulator.\n\n2. Comprehensive Comparison Table:\n\nA. Physical Properties:\n• Appearance & Luster: Metals have shiny metallic luster; Non-metals are dull (except shiny iodine and diamond).\n• Malleability & Ductility: Metals are malleable (sheets) and ductile (wires); Non-metals are brittle when solid, non-malleable, and non-ductile.\n• Electrical & Thermal Conductivity: Metals are excellent conductors (mobile delocalized electrons); Non-metals are poor conductors / insulators (except graphite).\n• Physical State at STP: Metals are all solids (except liquid Hg); Non-metals are gases (11), liquid (Br2), or solids (C, S, P, I2).\n• Melting & Boiling Points: Metals generally have high mp/bp; Non-metals generally have lower mp/bp (except carbon diamond/graphite).\n• Density: Metals have high densities; Non-metals have low densities.\n\nB. Chemical Properties:\n• Electron Behavior: Metals are electron donors (Reducing agents); Non-metals are electron acceptors (Oxidizing agents).\n• Nature of Oxides: Metals form basic oxides (e.g. Na2O, CaO) which form alkalies in water; Non-metals form acidic oxides (e.g. SO2, CO2) which form acids in water.\n• Reaction with Dilute Acids: Metals displace hydrogen gas from acids (Zn + 2HCl ──> ZnCl2 + H2); Non-metals do not react with dilute non-oxidizing acids.\n• Nature of Chlorides: Metals form electrovalent ionic chlorides (NaCl); Non-metals form covalent chlorides (PCl3, CCl4).\n• Hydrides: Metals form ionic hydrides (NaH, CaH2); Non-metals form stable covalent hydrides (CH4, NH3, H2O).",
