      "sloSq": [
        {
          "q": "1. (SLO) Explain why alkali metals (Group IA) are stored under kerosene oil while alkaline earth metals (Group IIA) like Magnesium can be stored in air.",
          "ans": "Alkali metals (Na, K) have extremely low ionization energies and react instantaneously and violently with oxygen and moisture in air, forming oxides/hydroxides and catching fire. In contrast, alkaline earth metals (Mg, Ca) have higher ionization energies and form a thin, impervious, protective oxide coating (e.g. MgO) on their surfaces upon exposure to air, which passivates the metal and prevents further rapid oxidation at room temperature."
        },
        {
          "q": "2. (SLO) Why does the chemical reactivity of alkali metals increase down the group (Li < Na < K < Cs), whereas the reactivity of halogens decreases down the group (F > Cl > Br > I)?",
          "ans": "• Alkali Metals react by LOSING electrons (electropositivity). Down Group IA, atomic size increases and ionization energy decreases, making it progressively easier to lose the valence electron; thus reactivity increases.\n• Halogens react by GAINING electrons (electronegativity). Down Group VIIA, atomic size increases and electron shielding increases, making it harder for the nucleus to attract an incoming electron; thus reactivity decreases."
        },
        {
          "q": "3. (SLO) Explain why Gold is considered the best metal for manufacturing electronic computer microprocessors despite its high cost.",
          "ans": "Gold is completely chemically inert and does not tarnish, oxidize, or corrode in any environment. It provides permanently clean, low-resistance, highly reliable electrical contacts for high-speed digital signals in microprocessors, preventing signal degradation or circuit failure over decades of use."
        },
        {
          "q": "4. (SLO) Differentiate between Duralumin and Magnalium alloys in terms of composition and applications.",
          "ans": "• Duralumin: Composed of Aluminium (95%), Copper (4%), Magnesium (0.5%), and Manganese (0.5%). It is lightweight, hard, and has high tensile strength, used extensively in aircraft frames and high-performance racing cars.\n• Magnalium: Composed of Aluminium (90-95%) and Magnesium (5-10%). It is corrosion-resistant, lightweight, and easily welded, used in optical instruments, scientific balances, and aircraft parts."
        }
      ],
      "sloLq": [
        {
          "q": "1. (SLO) Comprehensive Investigation: Analyze the chemical trends in Groups IA (Alkali metals), IIA (Alkaline earth metals), and VIIA (Halogens) with respect to atomic radius, ionization energy, electron affinity, electronegativity, and reaction mechanisms.",
          "ans": "1. Group IA (Alkali Metals):\n• Trends: Atomic radius increases (1.55 to 2.65 Å), Ionization energy decreases (520 to 376 kJ/mol), Electronegativity is lowest in table (1.0 to 0.7).\n• Mechanism: React exclusively by losing 1 electron (M ──> M+ + e-), acting as powerful reducing agents. Reactivity increases down the group (Li < Na < K < Rb < Cs).\n\n2. Group IIA (Alkaline Earth Metals):\n• Trends: Smaller radii than Group IA, Higher 1st IE (899 to 503 kJ/mol) and 2nd IE (1757 to 965 kJ/mol).\n• Mechanism: React by losing 2 electrons (M ──> M2+ + 2e-). Moderate reducing agents. Denser and harder than Group IA with higher melting points. Reactivity increases down the group (Be < Mg < Ca < Sr < Ba).\n\n3. Group VIIA (Halogens):\n• Trends: Atomic radius increases (71 to 150 pm), Electronegativity decreases (4.0 to 2.2), Electron affinity decreases (-349 to -270 kJ/mol).\n• Mechanism: React by gaining 1 electron (X2 + 2e- ──> 2X-), acting as powerful oxidizing agents. Reactivity decreases down the group (F2 > Cl2 > Br2 > I2). Exist in all three states of matter at STP (F2, Cl2 gases; Br2 liquid; I2 solid)."
        }
      ],
