              "num": "9.3.1",
              "name": "Conditions for Chemical Equilibrium",
              "desc": "To establish and preserve dynamic equilibrium in a chemical system, the following three conditions are essential:\n1. **Closed System:** The system must be sealed so that no reacting substance or product can enter or leave the system. In an open vessel, gases escape and equilibrium is impossible.\n2. **Constant Temperature:** The temperature of the system must remain unchanged. Because rate constants kf and kr vary with temperature, any change in temperature changes the numerical value of Kc.\n3. **Constant Pressure or Volume:** For reactions involving gaseous components, pressure and volume must remain constant because changes in pressure alter the concentrations of gases.",
              "sciNote": "Calcium carbonate decomposition: CaCO₃(s) ⇌ CaO(s) + CO₂(g). If heated in an open crucible, CO₂ gas escapes completely, driving the reaction to 100% completion (irreversible). If heated in a closed sealed vessel, CO₂ cannot escape and dynamic equilibrium is rapidly established.",
