            "q": "1. When a clear liquid is placed in a beaker, how can you identify it as an acid, base or neutral (water)?",
            "ans": "A clear liquid can be identified using the following systematic diagnostic tests:\n1. **Red and Blue Litmus Paper Test:**\n   - If blue litmus turns red, the liquid is an **Acid**.\n   - If red litmus turns blue, the liquid is a **Base (Alkali)**.\n   - If neither litmus paper changes colour, the liquid is **Neutral (pure water)**.\n2. **Universal Indicator Paper or pH Meter:**\n   - pH < 7 indicates an **Acid**.\n   - pH > 7 indicates a **Base**.\n   - pH = 7 exactly indicates **Neutral water**.\n3. **Baking Soda Test ($NaHCO_3$):** Adding a pinch of sodium bicarbonate causes effervescence ($CO_2$ gas bubbles) only in an acid."
