            "q": "2. Justify H⁺ ion as a Lewis acid?",
            "ans": "A hydrogen ion ($H^+$) is a bare proton with an empty 1s orbital and no valence electrons. According to Lewis theory, an acid is an electron pair acceptor.\n\nWhen $H^+$ reacts with an electron-rich species like ammonia ($:NH_3$) or water ($H_2\\ddot{O}:$), the $H^+$ ion accepts a lone pair of electrons to form a coordinate covalent (dative) bond:\n$H^+ + :NH_3 \\rightarrow [H \\leftarrow NH_3]^+ \\quad (NH_4^+)$\n$H^+ + H_2\\ddot{O}: \\rightarrow [H \\leftarrow OH_2]^+ \\quad (H_3O^+)$\nBecause $H^+$ accepts an electron pair, it fully satisfies the definition of a **Lewis acid**."
