              "name": "Modern Definition and Types of Organic Chemical Formulas",
              "desc": "**Modern Definition of Organic Chemistry:**\nOrganic chemistry is defined as the study of **hydrocarbons (compounds containing only carbon and hydrogen) and their derivatives**.\n*(Note: Compounds like $CO$, $CO_2$, carbonates, bicarbonates, and cyanides are classified as inorganic because their chemical behavior resembles mineral salts).*\n\n**Four Types of Organic Chemical Formulas:**\n1. **Molecular Formula:** Shows the actual number of atoms of each element in one molecule of a compound.\n   *Example for Butane:* $C_4H_{10}$.\n2. **Structural Formula:** Shows the exact arrangement of atoms and all covalent bonds connecting them in space.\n   *Example for Butane:*\n   ```text\n       H   H   H   H\n       |   |   |   |\n   H - C - C - C - C - H\n       |   |   |   |\n       H   H   H   H\n   ```\n3. **Condensed Formula:** Groups atoms connected to each carbon atom together sequentially without showing individual single bonds.\n   *Example for Butane:* $CH_3-CH_2-CH_2-CH_3$ or $CH_3(CH_2)_2CH_3$.\n4. **Dot-and-Cross (Electronic) Formula:** Depicts sharing of valence electrons between atoms using dots (•) and crosses (×) to show covalent bonds.",
