              "desc": "Open-chain organic compounds contain an open chain of carbon atoms where the terminal carbon atoms are not joined together:\n1. **Straight-Chain Compounds:** Carbon atoms are joined in a continuous single, unbranched line.\n   *Example:* n-Butane ($CH_3-CH_2-CH_2-CH_3$), n-Pentane ($CH_3-CH_2-CH_2-CH_2-CH_3$).\n2. **Branched-Chain Compounds:** One or more carbon side-chains are attached to the main carbon backbone.\n   *Example:* Isobutane (2-methylpropane):\n   ```text\n         CH3\n          |\n   CH3 - CH - CH3\n   ```\n   *Example:* Isopentane (2-methylbutane) and Neopentane (2,2-dimethylpropane).",
              "sciNote": "Acyclic compounds are also called aliphatic compounds (from Greek 'aleiphar' meaning fat) because early open-chain compounds were obtained from animal fats.",
              "examTip": "Straight-chain isomers are denoted with the prefix 'n-' (normal), single-branch with 'iso-', and quaternary central carbon with 'neo-'.",
