              "num": "11.4.1",
              "name": "Homologous Series",
              "desc": "A **homologous series** is a group or family of organic compounds having similar structural features and identical chemical properties, in which adjacent members differ from one another by a constant methylene group ($-CH_2-$) and 14 atomic mass units (a.m.u.).\n\n**Characteristics of a Homologous Series:**\n1. All members can be represented by a single **General Formula** (e.g., Alkanes: $C_nH_{2n+2}$; Alkenes: $C_nH_{2n}$; Alkynes: $C_nH_{2n-2}$).\n2. Any two consecutive members differ by a $-CH_2-$ (methylene) unit in composition and by $14 \\text{ g/mol}$ in molecular mass.\n3. All members possess the same **functional group**, giving them identical chemical properties.\n4. Physical properties (melting point, boiling point, density, viscosity) show a gradual, regular gradation as molecular mass increases.\n5. All members can be synthesized by similar general chemical methods.",
              "sciNote": "Why do members of a homologous series have the same chemical properties? Because chemical reactivity is dictated entirely by the functional group, which is identical for all members.",
              "examTip": "List the four main characteristics of a homologous series for 3-mark or 4-mark short questions.",
