            "q": "4. (a) Alkenes are unsaturated hydrocarbons. Explain the word unsaturated. (b) Describe the bonding between the two carbon atoms in Ethene. (c) Which functional group is present in alkenes? (d) Describe a simple chemical test to determine whether an unknown hydrocarbon is unsaturated or saturated.",
            "ans": "**(a) Meaning of Unsaturated:**\nIn organic chemistry, 'unsaturated' refers to compounds containing carbon-carbon multiple bonds (double or triple bonds) where carbon valencies are not fully satisfied by hydrogen atoms. They can add additional atoms across the multiple bonds without releasing any other atoms.\n\n**(b) Bonding between Carbon Atoms in Ethene ($CH_2=CH_2$):**\n- Both carbon atoms are $sp^2$-hybridized.\n- One $sp^2$ hybrid orbital of each carbon overlaps head-on to form a strong **$\\sigma$ (sigma) bond**.\n- The unhybridized $2p_z$ orbitals on both carbons overlap sideways (laterally) to form a weaker **$\\pi$ (pi) bond** whose electron cloud lies above and below the internuclear axis.\n- Total bond = 1 $\\sigma$ + 1 $\\pi$ bond, with bond angle of $120^\\circ$.\n\n**(c) Functional Group in Alkenes:**\nThe carbon-carbon double bond ($-C=C-$) is the functional group of alkenes.\n\n**(d) Simple Chemical Test for Unsaturation (Bromine Water Test):**\n- Take 2 cm³ of the unknown hydrocarbon in a test tube and add 1 cm³ of reddish-brown bromine water ($Br_2$ in $CCl_4$), then shake vigorously.\n- **Result for Unsaturated Hydrocarbon (Positive):** The reddish-brown colour of bromine is completely discharged, leaving a colourless solution ($CH_2=CH_2 + Br_2 \\rightarrow CH_2Br-CH_2Br$).\n- **Result for Saturated Hydrocarbon (Negative):** The reddish-brown colour persists unchanged under room conditions."
