              "num": "13.2.2",
              "name": "Denaturation and Uses of Proteins",
              "desc": "**Denaturation of Proteins:**\nDenaturation is the process in which a protein loses its specific three-dimensional native structure (tertiary and secondary folding) and biological activity due to physical or chemical changes, without breaking the primary covalent peptide bonds.\n- **Causes:** Heating (high temperature), extreme changes in pH (strong acids or bases), heavy metal ions ($Pb^{2+}, Hg^{2+}$), alcohol, and mechanical agitation.\n- *Common Everyday Example:* Boiling an egg. The clear, soluble liquid albumin protein in egg white coagulates irreversibly into an opaque, insoluble white solid when heated.\n\n**Sources and Functions of Proteins:**\n- **Sources:** Meat, fish, poultry, eggs, milk, cheese, lentils, pulses, beans, soybeans, and nuts.\n- **Biological Roles:**\n  1. *Enzymatic Catalysis:* All enzymes are proteins that catalyze biochemical reactions in cells (e.g. pepsin, amylase).\n  2. *Structural Support:* Keratin forms hair, nails, feathers, and horns; collagen provides tensile strength to skin, tendons, and bones.\n  3. *Transport:* Hemoglobin in red blood cells binds and transports oxygen from lungs to body tissues.\n  4. *Immunity:* Antibodies (immunoglobulins) identify and neutralize invading foreign bacteria and viruses.\n  5. *Regulation & Hormones:* Insulin regulates blood glucose levels.",
              "sciNote": "Cooking meat denatures tough muscle fibers and connective collagen, making the meat tender, digestible, and safe from bacterial contamination.",
              "examTip": "State clearly that denaturation destroys secondary and tertiary protein structures, but does NOT break primary covalent peptide bonds.",
