              "num": "13.3.1",
              "name": "Definition, Structure and Classification of Lipids",
              "desc": "**Definition:** Lipids are a diverse group of naturally occurring organic macromolecules that are insoluble in polar water but readily soluble in non-polar organic solvents (ether, chloroform, benzene, acetone).\n\n**Triglycerides (Structure):**\nThe most abundant lipids are **fats and oils**, which are chemically **triesters of glycerol and long-chain fatty acids (triglycerides)**:\n$\\text{Glycerol} + 3 \\text{ Fatty Acids} \\rightarrow \\text{Triglyceride} + 3H_2O$\n\n**Distinction Between Fats and Oils:**\n1. **Fats:**\n- Derived from **saturated fatty acids** (contain only single bonds in alkyl chains, e.g. stearic acid $C_{17}H_{35}COOH$, palmitic acid $C_{15}H_{31}COOH$).\n- Saturated hydrocarbon chains pack closely together, resulting in higher melting points.\n- **Solid or semi-solid at room temperature (25°C)**.\n- Chiefly of **animal origin** (butter, ghee, lard, tallow).\n\n2. **Oils:**\n- Derived from **unsaturated fatty acids** (contain one or more double bonds, e.g. oleic acid $C_{17}H_{33}COOH$, linoleic acid $C_{17}H_{31}COOH$).\n- Double bonds introduce kinks that prevent tight packing, lowering melting points.\n- **Liquid at room temperature (25°C)**.\n- Chiefly of **plant origin** (mustard oil, olive oil, sunflower oil, corn oil, cottonseed oil).\n\n**Hydrogenation of Oils (Ghee Manufacture):**\nUnsaturated liquid vegetable oils are converted into solid vegetable ghee (banaspati) by catalytic addition of hydrogen gas at 250°C in the presence of Nickel catalyst:\n$\\text{Vegetable Oil (Liquid)} + H_2 \\xrightarrow[250^\\circ\\text{C}]{Ni} \\text{Vegetable Ghee (Solid)}$",
              "sciNote": "Omega-3 and Omega-6 fatty acids found in fish oil and flaxseeds are essential polyunsaturated fatty acids that protect cardiovascular health.",
              "examTip": "In exams, remember: Fats = Saturated, Solid at room temp, Animal source. Oils = Unsaturated, Liquid at room temp, Plant source.",
