              "name": "Biological Functions of Lipids",
              "desc": "1. **Long-Term Energy Storage:** Lipids store more than **twice as much energy per gram ($38\\text{ kJ/g}$)** compared to carbohydrates or proteins ($17\\text{ kJ/g}$) due to their higher ratio of $C-H$ bonds.\n2. **Cell Membrane Structure:** Phospholipids and cholesterol form the fluid lipid bilayer matrix of all biological cell membranes.\n3. **Thermal Insulation:** Subcutaneous adipose tissue beneath the skin prevents bodily heat loss in cold environments (blubber in polar bears and whales).\n4. **Protection of Vital Organs:** Fat pads cushion and protect vital internal organs (kidneys, heart) against physical shock.\n5. **Absorption of Vitamins:** Essential for dissolving and transporting fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K) across the intestinal tract.",
