              "num": "13.4.1",
              "name": "Structure of Nucleic Acids (DNA and RNA)",
              "desc": "Nucleic acids are biopolymers essential for all known forms of life, originally isolated from cell nuclei by Swiss physician **Friedrich Miescher** in 1869.\n\n**Nucleotides (Building Blocks):**\nNucleic acids are composed of repeating monomer units called **nucleotides**. Each nucleotide is composed of three components:\n1. A **5-Carbon Pentose Sugar** (Deoxyribose in DNA, Ribose in RNA).\n2. A **Nitrogenous Base**:\n   - Purines: Adenine ($A$), Guanine ($G$)\n   - Pyrimidines: Cytosine ($C$), Thymine ($T$ - in DNA only), Uracil ($U$ - in RNA only).\n3. A **Phosphate Group ($-PO_4^{3-}$)**.\n\n**Two Types of Nucleic Acids:**\n\n1. **DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid):**\n- Discovered as a **Double Helix** by **James Watson and Francis Crick** in 1953.\n- Consists of two complementary anti-parallel polynucleotide strands coiled around a central axis like a twisted ladder.\n- The sugar-phosphate backbone forms the outer rails, and nitrogenous base pairs form the internal rungs linked by specific **hydrogen bonds**:\n  - Adenine pairs strictly with Thymine ($A = T$) via 2 hydrogen bonds.\n  - Guanine pairs strictly with Cytosine ($G \\equiv C$) via 3 hydrogen bonds.\n- Contains the sugar **2-deoxyribose**.\n\n2. **RNA (Ribonucleic Acid):**\n- Consists of a **single polynucleotide strand**.\n- Contains the sugar **ribose**.\n- Contains **Uracil ($U$)** instead of Thymine ($A-U$ pairing).\n- Three functional types: Messenger RNA (mRNA), Transfer RNA (tRNA), and Ribosomal RNA (rRNA).",
              "sciNote": "If the DNA in a single human cell were uncoiled and stretched out end-to-end, it would measure about 2 meters in length!",
              "examTip": "Remember complementary base pairing rules: A pairs with T (2 H-bonds) and G pairs with C (3 H-bonds) in DNA. In RNA, Uracil (U) replaces Thymine.",
