            "ans": "**(a) The Four Layers of the Atmosphere:**\n1. **Troposphere (0–12 km):** Contains 80% of atmospheric mass and all weather phenomena. Temperature drops from $+15^\\circ\\text{C}$ to $-56^\\circ\\text{C}$ at the tropopause.\n2. **Stratosphere (12–50 km):** Contains the ozone shield (25–30 km). Temperature increases from $-56^\\circ\\text{C}$ to $-2^\\circ\\text{C}$ at the stratopause.\n3. **Mesosphere (50–85 km):** Coldest layer, temperature drops from $-2^\\circ\\text{C}$ to $-93^\\circ\\text{C}$. Meteors burn up here.\n4. **Thermosphere (85–120+ km):** Temperature rises rapidly above $1200^\\circ\\text{C}$; contains ionized gases reflecting radio waves.\n\n```text\nAltitude (km)\n  120+ |  THERMOSPHERE  (T > 1200°C) [Ionosphere]\n       |========================================== Mesopause\n   85  |  MESOSPHERE    (T drops to -93°C)\n       |========================================== Stratopause\n   50  |  STRATOSPHERE  (T rises to -2°C) [*** OZONE LAYER 25-30 km ***]\n       |========================================== Tropopause\n   12  |  TROPOSPHERE   (T drops to -56°C) [Weather, clouds, life]\n    0  +------------------------------------------ Earth Surface (+15°C)\n```\n\n**(b) Comparison between Troposphere and Stratosphere:**\n| Feature | Troposphere | Stratosphere |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Altitude** | 0 to 12 km | 12 to 50 km |\n| **Temperature Trend** | Decreases with height (lapse rate: $-6.5^\\circ\\text{C/km}$) | Increases with height (rises to $-2^\\circ\\text{C}$) |\n| **Ozone Concentration** | Negligible (acts as pollutant if present) | Maximum concentration (protective ozone shield) |\n| **Weather Phenomena** | All clouds, storms, rainfall occur here | Dry, peaceful, cloudless (ideal for jet airplanes) |\n| **Mass of Atmosphere** | Contains 75% to 80% of total atmospheric air | Contains about 19% of atmospheric air |"
