              "name": "Removal of Permanent Hardness (Washing Soda & Ion Exchange)",
              "desc": "Permanent hardness ($CaCl_2, CaSO_4, MgCl_2, MgSO_4$) requires chemical precipitation or ion exchange:\n\n1. **By Washing Soda (Sodium Carbonate, $Na_2CO_3$):**\nWhen sodium carbonate is added to permanent hard water, it reacts with both soluble calcium and magnesium sulphates/chlorides to precipitate insoluble carbonates:\n$CaSO_4(aq) + Na_2CO_3(aq) \\rightarrow CaCO_3(s) \\downarrow + Na_2SO_4(aq)$\n$CaCl_2(aq) + Na_2CO_3(aq) \\rightarrow CaCO_3(s) \\downarrow + 2NaCl(aq)$\n$MgSO_4(aq) + Na_2CO_3(aq) \\rightarrow MgCO_3(s) \\downarrow + Na_2SO_4(aq)$\n*(Note: Washing soda also removes temporary hardness: $Ca(HCO_3)_2 + Na_2CO_3 \\rightarrow CaCO_3 \\downarrow + 2NaHCO_3$).*\n\n2. **By Ion-Exchange Method (Sodium Zeolite Process):**\n- **Zeolite (Permutit):** A natural or synthetic hydrated sodium aluminium silicate, chemically represented as **$Na_2O\\cdot Al_2O_3\\cdot 2SiO_2\\cdot xH_2O$**, simplified as **$Na_2Z$**.\n- **Process:** Hard water is percolated down a tall cylinder packed with insoluble sodium zeolite granules. The zeolite captures divalent $Ca^{2+}$ and $Mg^{2+}$ ions from water and releases non-hardening $Na^+$ ions into solution:\n$Na_2Z(s) + Ca^{2+}(aq) \\rightarrow CaZ(s) + 2Na^+(aq)$\n$Na_2Z(s) + Mg^{2+}(aq) \\rightarrow MgZ(s) + 2Na^+(aq)$\n- **Regeneration of Exhausted Zeolite:** When all sodium ions in the resin are exhausted, the zeolite bed is washed with a concentrated solution of **brine ($10\\%\\text{ aqueous } NaCl$)**, restoring its softening capacity:\n$CaZ(s) + 2NaCl(aq) \\rightarrow Na_2Z(s) + CaCl_2(aq)$\nThe soluble $CaCl_2$ is washed down the drain, and the regenerated $Na_2Z$ is ready for reuse.",
              "sciNote": "Modern domestic water softeners and laboratory deionizers use synthetic organic ion-exchange resin beads that can remove all mineral ions completely, producing demineralized (ultrapure) water.",
              "examTip": "Write the zeolite exchange reaction ($Na_2Z + Ca^{2+} \\rightarrow CaZ + 2Na^+$) and the regeneration reaction with $NaCl$ ($CaZ + 2NaCl \\rightarrow Na_2Z + CaCl_2$) for full marks.",
