              "num": "16.3.1",
              "name": "Raw Materials and Synthesis Reactions of Urea",
              "desc": "**Urea ($NH_2CONH_2$):**\nA white crystalline organic solid containing **$46.67\\%$ nitrogen**, making it the most concentrated and widely utilized solid nitrogenous fertilizer in world agriculture.\n\n**Raw Materials:**\n1. **Liquid Ammonia ($NH_3$):** Synthesized industrially by the Haber-Bosch process ($N_2 + 3H_2 \\rightleftharpoons 2NH_3$).\n2. **Carbon Dioxide gas ($CO_2$):** Obtained as a byproduct from the steam reforming of natural gas in ammonia plants.\n\n**Manufacturing Steps & Reactions:**\n1. **Reaction of Ammonia and CO₂ (Formation of Ammonium Carbamate):**\nLiquid ammonia and carbon dioxide are compressed into a high-pressure reactor at **$180–200^\\circ\\text{C}$ and $150–200\\text{ atmospheres}$ pressure** to form ammonium carbamate:\n$2NH_3(l) + CO_2(g) \\xrightarrow{180-200^\\circ\\text{C}, 200\\text{ atm}} \\underset{\\text{Ammonium Carbamate}}{NH_2-COO-NH_4}$\nThis reaction is highly exothermic.\n\n2. **Dehydration of Ammonium Carbamate:**\nThe carbamate is fed into a decomposer where pressure is reduced; it undergoes endothermic dehydration to form molten urea:\n$NH_2COONH_4 \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\underset{\\text{Urea}}{NH_2-CO-NH_2} + H_2O$\n\n3. **Evaporation and Concentration:**\nThe liquid urea solution ($~80\\%$ urea) is concentrated in vacuum evaporators to remove water, yielding a $99.7\\%$ molten urea melt.\n\n4. **Prilling (Prilling Tower):**\nMolten urea is pumped to the top of a tall cooling tower (prilling tower) and sprayed downward through rotating prilling shower nozzles. A counter-current upward stream of cold air cools the droplets, solidifying them into uniform, dry, spherical white pellets called **urea prills**.",
              "sciNote": "Prills are coated with a minute film of conditioning clay or formaldehyde to prevent the hygroscopic urea granules from caking together during humid storage.",
              "examTip": "Write both reactions: (1) 2NH₃ + CO₂ → NH₂COONH₄, and (2) NH₂COONH₄ → NH₂CONH₂ + H₂O.",
              "concepts": [
                "Raw materials: Ammonia (NH₃) and Carbon dioxide (CO₂).",
                "Step 1: 2NH₃ + CO₂ → NH₂COONH₄ (ammonium carbamate).",
                "Step 2: NH₂COONH₄ → NH₂CONH₂ (urea) + H₂O.",
                "Prilling tower solidifies urea melt into spherical prills."
              ]
